               <td>0.036</td>\n    74│                 <td>97.29</td>\n    75│                 <td>93.42</td>\n    76│                 <td>95.92</td>\n    77│                 <td>0.120</td>\n    78│             </tr>\n    79│         </tbody>\n    80│     </table>\n    81│ \n    82│ \n    83│ ### 🏆 Unmatched SOTA Performance\n    84│ **MinerU2.5-Pro** is our latest document parsing model (PDF-to-Markdown) that establishes a new industry standard. By focusing entirely on data engineering without altering the original 1.2B-parameter architecture, it delivers exceptional results across the board:\n    85│ \n    86│ **1. Defeating Leading Models on OmniDocBench v1.6**\n    87│ On the newly proposed, highly rigorous OmniDocBench v1.6, MinerU2.5-Pro achieves the absolute **SOTA overall score of 95.69**. It comprehensively outperforms both top-tier specialized OCR models (**GLM-OCR, PaddleOCR-VL-1.5**) and massive frontier VLMs (**Gemini 3 Pro, Qwen3-VL-235B**). \n    88│ \n    89│ **2. Massive Leap from MinerU 2.5 via Data Engineering**\n    90│ Compared to the previous MinerU 2.5 baseline, the overall score skyrocketed from **92.98 to 95.69**. This breakthrough was achieved not by scaling model parameters, but through meticulous data engineering—drastically expanding data scale, enriching distribution and difficulty diversity, and systematically elevating annotation quality.\n    91│ \n    92│ **3. Exceptional Modality-Specific Breakthroughs**\n    93│ *   📊 **Table Parsing:** Evaluated across 5 diverse table benchmarks, MinerU2.5-Pro dominates the leaderboard. It outperforms the 2nd place model by **1.39 points** and surpasses the original MinerU by **3.06 points** (with Table TEDS jumping specifically by **+5.54** on OmniDocBench).\n    94│ *   🧮 **Formulas & Text:** Achieves SOTA levels with Dense Formula parsing (CDM) reaching **97.29** (+1.70), and Text Edit Distance dropping to an industry-best **0.036**.\n    95│ \n    96│ **4. ✨ New Practical Capabilities**\n    97│ Beyond metric improvements, MinerU2.5-Pro now natively supports: **Image & Chart Parsing**, **Truncated Paragraph Merging**, **Cross-Page Table Merging** and **In-Table Image Recognition**.\n    98│ \n    99│ ---\n   100│ \n   101│ ### 💡 How We Achieved It: The Data Engine\n   102│ Current SOTA models (regardless of architecture) consistently fail on the same set of complex layouts. We realized the true bottleneck is **training data deficiency and annotation noise**. To fix this, we built a novel Data Engine:\n   103│ \n   104│ 1.  **Difficulty & Diversity-Aware Scaling:** We expanded the training corpus from under 10M to **65.5M pages**, heavily targeting long-tail hard samples while controlling distribution shifts.\n   105│ 2.  **Solving the \"Annotation Paradox\":** Complex tables and dense formulas usually suffer from noisy automatic labels. We generated ultra-reliable annotations using **Cross-Model Consistency Verification (CMCV)** and an iterative **Judge-and-Refine** pipeline.\n   106│ 3.  **3-Stage Progressive Training:** We maximized data utility by matching data quality tiers to a structured training pipeline: Large-scale Pre-training ➡️ High-quality Hard-Sample Fine-Tuning ➡️ GRPO Format Alignment.\n   107│ \n   108│ **Bottom Line:** MinerU2.5-Pro proves that systematic data engineering is the ultimate lever for document parsing, providing the most accurate structural extraction available today for LLM data pipelines and advanced RAG systems.\n   109│ \n   110│ \n   111│ \n   112│ # 1. Quick Start\n   113│ For convenience, we provide `mineru-vl-utils`, a Python package that simplifies the process of sending requests and handling responses from MinerU2.5-Pro Vision-Language Model. Here we give some examples to use MinerU2.5-Pro. For more information and usages, please refer to [mineru-vl-utils](https://github.com/opendatalab/mineru-vl-utils/tree/main).\n   114│ \n   115│ 📌 We strongly recommend using vllm for inference, as the `vllm-async-engine` can achieve a concurrent inference speed of **2.12 fps** on one A100.\n   116│ \n   117│ ## 1.1. Install packages\n   118│ ```bash\n   119│ # For `transformers` backend\n   120│ pip install \"mineru-vl-utils[transformers]\"\n   121│ # For `vllm-engine` and `vllm-async-engine` backend\n   122│ pip install \"mineru-vl-utils[vllm]\"\n   123│ ```\n   124│ \n   125│ ## 1.2. `transformers` Example\n   126│ \n   127│ ```python\n   128│ from transformers import AutoProcessor, Qwen2VLForConditionalGeneration\n   129│ from PIL import Image\n   130│ from mineru_vl_utils import MinerUClient\n   131│ \n   132│ # for transformers>=4.56.0\n   133│ model = Qwen2VLForConditionalGeneration.from_pretrained(\n   134│     \"opendatalab/MinerU2.5-Pro-2604-1.2B\", dtype=\"auto\", device_map=\"auto\"\n   135│ )\n   136│ \n   137│ processor = AutoProcessor.from_pretrained(\n   138│     \"opendatalab/MinerU2.5-Pro-2604-1.2B\", use_fast=True\n   139│ )\n   140│ \n   141│ client = MinerUClient(\n   142│     backend=\"transformers\", model=model, processor=processor,\n   143│     image_analysis=False # default False, set True to enable image/chart analysis\n   144│ )\n   145│ \n   146│ print(client.two_step_extract(Image.open(\"/path/to/page.png\")))\n   147│ ```\n   148│ \n   149│ ## 1.3. `vllm-engine` Example (Recommended!)\n   150│ \n   151│ ```python\n   152│ from vllm import LLM\n   153│ from PIL import Image\n   154│ from mineru_vl_utils import MinerUClient\n   155│ from mineru_vl_utils import MinerULogitsProcessor  # if vllm>=0.10.1\n   156│ \n   157│ llm = LLM(\n   158│     model=\"opendatalab/MinerU2.5-Pro-2604-1.2B\",\n   159│     logits_processors=[MinerULogitsProcessor]  # if vllm>=0.10.1\n   160│ )\n   161│ \n   162│ client = MinerUClient(\n   163│     backend=\"vllm-engine\", vllm_llm=llm,\n   164│     image_analysis=False # default False, set True to enable image/chart analysis\n   165│ )\n   166│ \n   167│ print(client.two_step_extract(Image.open(\"/path/to/page.png\")))\n   168│ ```\n   169│ \n   170│ ## 1.4. JSON result to Markdown (enable truncated paragraph merging)\n   171│ \n   172│ ```python\n   173│ from mineru_vl_utils.post_process import json2md\n   174│ \n   175│ # ... omit client initialize\n   176│ content_list = client.two_step_extract(Image.open(\"path/to/page.png\"))\n   177│ md_res = json2md(content_list)\n   178│ ```\n   179│ \n   180│ 🚧 Cross-Page Table Merging: Currently under integration. Stay tuned!\n   181│ \n   182│ \n   183│ # 2. Performance\n   184│ ## 2.1. End-to-End Document Parsing on OmniDocBench v1.6\n   185│ <p align=\"left\">\n   186│     <img alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://hotelll.github.io/MinerU2.5-Pro/end2end.png\"/>\n   187│ <p>\n   188│ \n   189│ ## 2.2. Text Recognition\n   190│ <p align=\"left\">\n   191│     <img alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://hotelll.github.io/MinerU2.5-Pro/text_performance.png\" style=\"max-width:60%; height:auto;\" />\n   192│ </p>\n   193│ \n   194│ ## 2.3. Formula Recognition\n   195│ <p align=\"left\">\n   196│     <img alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://hotelll.github.io/MinerU2.5-Pro/formula_performance.png\" style=\"max-width:80%; height:auto;\" />\n   197│ <p>\n   198│ \n   199│ ## 2.4. Table Recognition\n   200│ <p align=\"left\">\n\n[TRUNCATED] Showing lines 1-200 of 251. To continue, call read_file with path=\"/root/.magic-pdf/models/MinerU2.5-Pro/README.md\" start_line=201 max_lines=200\n</file>"
